# The CTL* fragment: path quantifiers A and E, and semantic negation.
#
# See demos/README.md for how to run this script.
## Every verdict here goes through ltlsynt (from Spot) on the PATH.

# demo_6.1 switches the fragment on and decides two formulas. This demo asks
# what A, E and the semantic negation - mean for synthesis. A formula with
# A or E is rejected until the fragment is switched:

fragment ltl
sat A F (o1[t]:bv[1] = 1)

fragment ctl_star

# A quantifies over all paths of the environment's inputs, E over some path.
# For an output the system chooses, A F says the system can make it 1 at some
# point whatever the inputs do, and E G says some path keeps it at 0:

sat A F (o1[t]:bv[1] = 1)
sat E G (o1[t]:bv[1] = 0)

# realizable asks whether the system can win. It cannot force an input, so A F
# over an input is unrealizable, while E F over the same input is realizable:
# some path carries the 1.

realizable A F (i1[t]:bv[1] = 1)
realizable E F (i1[t]:bv[1] = 1)
realizable A F (o1[t]:bv[1] = 1)

# The semantic negation - is not the Boolean complement. - phi says phi is
# unrealizable: the environment can always violate it. The environment cannot
# be forced to raise i1, so F i1 = 1 is unrealizable and its - holds. The
# system can keep o1 at 1 forever, so G o1 = 1 is realizable and its - does not:

realizable -(G (o1[t]:bv[1] = 1))
realizable -(F (i1[t]:bv[1] = 1))

# It prints as written and is a closed statement, so it folds to the same
# constant under a temporal operator:

n -(G (o1[t]:bv[1] = 1))
sat G (-(F (i1[t]:bv[1] = 1)))

# A formula with A, E or - is reduced to an LTL problem with one witness
# output per existential choice, so sat and realizable work as for LTL. valid
# does not decide such a formula and answers UNKNOWN, so it is not run here.

# run plays a strategy. Under A the outputs answer every input, as in an LTL
# run; this one mirrors a file of inputs:

i1:bv[1] := in file("fixture/lookback-bv1.in")
run 4 steps A G (o1[t]:bv[1] = i1[t]:bv[1])
stop

# Without the fragment these operators are an error again:

fragment ltl
realizable -(G (o1[t]:bv[1] = 1))

quit
